[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186074189 :question/problem "![](images/2c/2c9f6bba4d2a0e1b1499a8813a2035f838113cbcb5e0fe20c219f45bf011c471.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $\\overset{⟷}{PQ}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{MN}.$" :question/worked-solution "By the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point on the perpendicular bisector of a segment is equidistant from the endpoints of the segment. Hence, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QM & =QN \\\\ 2x-3 & =3x-10 \\\\ -3 & =x-10 \\\\ 7 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186071372 :answer-field/correct 17592186071377 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073878 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-76f9e40b-bbe6-52c0-88f8-30c8770f890f" "question-8e4299a8-dbfe-5e92-9aa9-392339c4eb42" "question-d3857009-5cce-53bf-a9fe-94834655a415"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-76f9e40b-bbe6-52c0-88f8-30c8770f890f" :question/id #uuid "76f9e40b-bbe6-52c0-88f8-30c8770f890f" :question/math-academy-id "q-53023" :question/problem "![](images/fa/fae19bb8db103c78bf70bfb65d807f18df344264606282c7a56392a659012f8a.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $VY=WY$ and $VZ=WZ.$" :question/worked-solution "By the converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point equidistant from the endpoints of a segment lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment.\n\nHere, the points $Y$ and $Z$ are both equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{VW}.$ Therefore, $Y$ and $Z$ both  lie on the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{VW}.$    \n\nSince only one line can be drawn through two given points, the line $\\overset{⟷}{YZ}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{VW}.$ \n\nIn particular, we have that $VX=WX.$ We can use this equation to solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}VX & =WX \\\\ 4x-2 & =2x+4 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4925edad-9c11-5801-8130-112567572927"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4925edad-9c11-5801-8130-112567572927" :answer-field/id #uuid "4925edad-9c11-5801-8130-112567572927" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cdc6029e-345c-5e41-bdab-a7033158ba04" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e77b8f9c-6465-5f4e-a96d-ba912d1aa757" "answer-b0e8081a-379c-5567-b273-bf5409da9649" "answer-cdc6029e-345c-5e41-bdab-a7033158ba04" "answer-bc5bb86f-5f82-514f-ad63-9a8578416198" "answer-be6179a7-3097-5ceb-bc0d-4ffec175d922"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e77b8f9c-6465-5f4e-a96d-ba912d1aa757" :answer/id #uuid "e77b8f9c-6465-5f4e-a96d-ba912d1aa757" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0e8081a-379c-5567-b273-bf5409da9649" :answer/id #uuid "b0e8081a-379c-5567-b273-bf5409da9649" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cdc6029e-345c-5e41-bdab-a7033158ba04" :answer/id #uuid "cdc6029e-345c-5e41-bdab-a7033158ba04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc5bb86f-5f82-514f-ad63-9a8578416198" :answer/id #uuid "bc5bb86f-5f82-514f-ad63-9a8578416198" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be6179a7-3097-5ceb-bc0d-4ffec175d922" :answer/id #uuid "be6179a7-3097-5ceb-bc0d-4ffec175d922" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8e4299a8-dbfe-5e92-9aa9-392339c4eb42" :question/id #uuid "8e4299a8-dbfe-5e92-9aa9-392339c4eb42" :question/math-academy-id "q-76794" :question/problem "![](images/f0/f07faaa85e166b53068db13609fd10958dfe4857df5118188be3ca709e6e0b8b.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $KN=LN$ and $KP=LP.$" :question/worked-solution "By the converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point equidistant from the endpoints of a segment lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment. \n\nHere, the points $P$ and $N$ are both equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ Therefore, $P$ and $N$ both  lie on the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ \n\nSince only one line can be drawn through two given points, the line $\\overset{⟷}{PN}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ \n\nIn particular, we have that $KM=LM.$ We can use this equation to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}KM & =LM \\\\ 2x+2 & =4x-6 \\\\ -2x & =-8 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bc772387-bb72-5d5b-abe3-ac9a76d448eb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bc772387-bb72-5d5b-abe3-ac9a76d448eb" :answer-field/id #uuid "bc772387-bb72-5d5b-abe3-ac9a76d448eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7db77d85-a0dc-5dc2-a4db-a208a7e4e24b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-824ef6d0-40cc-5ffb-90ae-9f5f0cac548b" "answer-7db77d85-a0dc-5dc2-a4db-a208a7e4e24b" "answer-0a509626-e980-52d1-8ac6-b779e420ad7c" "answer-5595a2b1-93ac-5938-9351-2e8f17025a42" "answer-1fe152df-c056-56e4-8835-05f63b7ca375"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-824ef6d0-40cc-5ffb-90ae-9f5f0cac548b" :answer/id #uuid "824ef6d0-40cc-5ffb-90ae-9f5f0cac548b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7db77d85-a0dc-5dc2-a4db-a208a7e4e24b" :answer/id #uuid "7db77d85-a0dc-5dc2-a4db-a208a7e4e24b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a509626-e980-52d1-8ac6-b779e420ad7c" :answer/id #uuid "0a509626-e980-52d1-8ac6-b779e420ad7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5595a2b1-93ac-5938-9351-2e8f17025a42" :answer/id #uuid "5595a2b1-93ac-5938-9351-2e8f17025a42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fe152df-c056-56e4-8835-05f63b7ca375" :answer/id #uuid "1fe152df-c056-56e4-8835-05f63b7ca375" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d3857009-5cce-53bf-a9fe-94834655a415" :question/id #uuid "d3857009-5cce-53bf-a9fe-94834655a415" :question/math-academy-id "q-76796" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c58a97e70d389bda3095be742a5cc4ce1ef7dc2691a7841f8e6204d87c4cf5e2.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $KN=LN$ and $KP=LP.$" :question/worked-solution "By the converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point equidistant from the endpoints of a segment lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment. \n\nHere, the points $P$ and $N$ are both equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ Therefore, $P$ and $N$ both  lie on the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ \n\nSince only one line can be drawn through two given points, the line $\\overset{⟷}{PN}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ \n\nIn particular, we have that $KM=LM.$ We can use this equation to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}KM & =LM \\\\ 9x & =6x+9 \\\\ 3x & =9 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6b0db482-278a-592b-bf99-1c44d23615a5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6b0db482-278a-592b-bf99-1c44d23615a5" :answer-field/id #uuid "6b0db482-278a-592b-bf99-1c44d23615a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cabcbe23-bc92-53bc-ad3b-2cf4a4783d39" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ebe569bc-fee7-5ea3-817e-0ce3b60d2946" "answer-cabcbe23-bc92-53bc-ad3b-2cf4a4783d39" "answer-6ba1e665-96d2-5e2e-bfb6-4dc5353d2e5d" "answer-77dea43e-858b-53a5-9a64-8090da8616a5" "answer-c739e